<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Kilij Arslan I</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1079–1107</span> · <a href="/centuries/11th-century">11th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Kilij Arslan I was sultan of the Seljuk <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultanate-of-rum">Sultanate of Rum</a> from 1092 to 1107. He destroyed the People's <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crusade">Crusade</a> in 1096 but lost his capital <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nicaea">Nicaea</a> to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-crusade">First Crusade</a> in 1097 and was defeated at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dorylaeum">Dorylaeum</a>. He rebuilt his state in central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anatolia">Anatolia</a> and drowned in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khabur-river">Khabur</a> river in 1107 after a defeat in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesopotamia">Mesopotamia</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kilij-arslan-i.jpg" alt="Kilij Arslan I" width="320" height="388" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Kusadasi500 · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kilicaslan_heykel.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Kilij Arslan, whose name means Sword Lion, was the son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suleiman-ibn-qutalmish">Suleiman ibn Qutalmish</a>. After his father's death in 1086 he became a hostage of the Great Seljuq sultan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malik-shah-i">Malik Shah I</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isfahan">Isfahan</a>, and was released when Malik Shah died in 1092 in the wake of a quarrel among his jailers. He then marched at the head of the Turkish Oghuz Yiva tribe army and set up his capital at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nicaea">Nicaea</a>. After Malik Shah's death the Danishmends, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/artuqid-dynasty">Artuqids</a> and other tribes began to compete to found independent states. Kilij Arslan married Ayşe Hatun, daughter of the emir <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tzachas">Tzachas</a>, and in 1094 killed his father-in-law at a banquet in his tent at Smyrna.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>When the People's Crusade of Peter the Hermit and Walter the Penniless reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nicaea">Nicaea</a> in 1096, Kilij Arslan sent spies to tempt the crusaders to rush to the fort of Xerigordos and starved them out; those who renounced <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a> were spared and sent east as captives, and the rest were put to death. Near the village of Civetot his army surprised the remaining crusaders, almost entirely unarmed civilians, and around 17,000 of 20,000 were killed.</p><p>Believing the main army no threat, he went to war with the Danishmends and was away when the crusaders besieged <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nicaea">Nicaea</a> in May 1097. He was defeated on 21 May, the city surrendered to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantines</a> and his wife and children were captured. Allied with the Danishmends, he planned an ambush near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dorylaeum">Dorylaeum</a>, but his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/horse-archer">horse archers</a> could not break the line of the crusader knights, and Bohemond's main body captured the Turkish camp on 1 July. In 1101 he defeated crusader armies at Mersivan and Heraclea Cybistra and moved his capital to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/konya">Konya</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crusade">crusades</a> he moved east, taking <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harran">Harran</a> and Diyarbakr. In 1107 Ghazaghli, castellan of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mosul">Mosul</a>'s citadel, asked for his help against Jawali Saqawa, the atabeg appointed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-i-tapar">Muhammad I Tapar</a>. Kilij Arslan entered Mosul, appointed his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malik-shah-i">Malik Shah</a> ruler and set out to fight Jawali near the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khabur-river">Khabur River</a>. His army was destroyed. Believing he would not be forgiven for challenging Sultan Muhammad, he rode into the river, shooting arrows at his pursuers, until his horse sank and he drowned. In January 2021 archaeologists from Dicle University identified what they took to be his grave and that of his daughter Saide Hatun at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mayyafariqin">Silvan</a>, Diyarbakır.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Kilij Arslan I” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>His son <b>Kilij Arslan I</b>, held captive by Sultan Malik-Shah, was released after Malik-Shah’s death in 1092 and returned to Nicaea.</blockquote><a href="/empire/sultanate-of-rum">Sultanate of Rum · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The First Crusade routed the army of <b>Kilij Arslan I</b> at Dorylaeum in Anatolia.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/july-1">1 July in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Alexios I persuaded Chaka's son-in-law, the Rum Seljuk <b>Kilij Arslan I</b>, to kill him at a banquet around 1093.</blockquote><a href="/state/beylik-of-chaka">Beylik of Chaka</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khabur-river">Khabur River</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p></section>
